ore, $24={2}^{3}\\cdot 3.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\ 28 & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 7 \\\\ 24 & ={2}^{3}\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $3$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $21$, $28$  and $24$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(21,28,24)={2}^{3}\\cdot 3\\cdot 7=168.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "746e99f3-561d-5212-ba2a-3d34b8bcdbee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Three Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3059e99-3f5b-5844-8f15-e388da08df46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3059e99-3f5b-5844-8f15-e388da08df46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493725} :answer-field/id #uuid "962cde6c-5ec7-5c5b-8f76-f152a51a0365" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42ddf56f-a8d0-56db-815a-a130a857fd56" :question/math-academy-id "q-171159" :question/problem "$\\text{LCM}(9,15,2)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- First, we find the prime factorization of $9.$ We easily deduce that $9={3}^{2}.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $15.$ We easily deduce that $15=3\\times 5.$\n\n- Then, we notice that $2$ is a prime number.\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 15 & =3\\times 5 \\\\ 2 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $9$, $15,$ and $2$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(9,15,2)=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 5=90.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384f8f04-972d-50cb-a023-dba29924242e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Two Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e5d67ca-c6be-58ec-93e1-49682dfb7816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "350" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081072} {:answer/id #uuid "2421020d-932b-5f28-8f95-accb6c2498b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "490" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081075} {:answer/id #uuid "27b02112-73bf-53d0-a6f3-e65099f6f3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493455} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcce3aa-fa33-5496-98f8-707499c7ea27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,430" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493611} {:answer/id #uuid "e24b5eac-3f91-5144-8391-b6b9afa76ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e24b5eac-3f91-5144-8391-b6b9afa76ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493749} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8a35835-75d5-5fbb-9a3c-74eb04a5a5e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4df64354-ee14-501d-92ab-db3871c23e35" :question/math-academy-id "q-40529" :question/problem "$\\text{LCM}(98,175)=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the lowest common multiple of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $98:$ \n\n![](images/c7/c74979d4af6e34328dca1fab75fbf07c138c28ec8b93d5462d889f08ac6ea6c0.png)\n\nTherefore, $98=2\\cdot {7}^{2}.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $175:$\n\n![](images/56/56e885903dd09097a256b6740f7f6307ba2af3c0e43f32bb6456745acfb3e777.png)\n\n \nTherefore, $175=7\\cdot {5}^{2}.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}98 & =2\\cdot {7}^{2} \\\\ 175 & =7\\cdot {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $2$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $98$ and $175$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(98,175)=2\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2}=2\\,450.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "746e99f3-561d-5212-ba2a-3d34b8bcdbee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Three Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ba87deb-540d-521e-8013-35015385eaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493439} {:answer/id #uuid "45483506-27c3-5ae9-8704-9d