=2\\cdot 7 \\\\ 18 & =2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ 63 & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $14$, $18$  and $63$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(14,18,63)=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 7=126.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083331 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28dd57d-e6e5-5704-94a4-5f9bfb37f383" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Denominator of Two Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499c5d09-2f7f-51d6-82b6-ddaf516d1430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "580" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493515} {:answer/id #uuid "56a59234-02a6-5ffb-861d-20a6d3123bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493523} {:answer/id #uuid "849c6e56-e751-5252-952f-3302adb354ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,920" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493600} {:answer/id #uuid "ac29b778-5c98-56c7-b58e-e5939e06fb60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493661} {:answer/id #uuid "db9cc569-ed07-58d2-a31c-6b23e8a5cefd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac29b778-5c98-56c7-b58e-e5939e06fb60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493661} :answer-field/id #uuid "727d9f99-59b5-57e0-acfe-f77120569223" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e11aa68-986c-58e0-bcdf-2852036febc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-7317" :question/problem "Find the lowest common denominator of $\\frac{11}{30}$ and $\\frac{19}{64}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $30:$\n\n![](images/31/312d8c33355fb36260cce172ab20bf907637528d0a5d04dc4dd1eceb59d765dd.png)\n\nTherefore, $30=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $64:$\n\n![](images/44/447dca25b6cd3e19abd115869dac18c66b6066a37ef6181b4ac68e478aafb0b8.png)\n\nTherefore, $64={2}^{6}.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 64 & ={2}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $6$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is ${2}^{6}\\cdot 3\\cdot 5=960.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384f8f04-972d-50cb-a023-dba29924242e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Two Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0846cba1-214f-563e-8599-5c89551c8b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493403} {:answer/id #uuid "0a872d5e-744c-5b09-8f5b-a437fac96cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493